ight  they may not spawn the next morning so  same thing with a temperature drop all  sorts of these kinds of sort of acute  environmental irregularities can be a  big problem  photo period we just talked about that  disease is a huge one okay so I've been  in situations where there's been an  acute outbreak of a disease fish  typically in some cases we've had fish  dying from disease and fish other fish  are still spawning within the same tanks  but if the event is acute enough and the  agent is acute enough they are not gonna  spawn okay so this is something that  could be an issue so if there's some  kind of pathogen that's that's sweeping  through your populations you're gonna  know it anyways  chances are but certainly then it's  going to impact spawning as well in the  short term so what you've got here is  sort of this overview now again what I  hope to have Illustrated to you  over the course of this talk is one how  complex this is it's not a cut and dry  issue - I've hope to have Illustrated to  you that there is a pathway that you  should be following a logical  progression of questions that you should  be asking when you're trying to  troubleshoot green and then finally I'm  hoping that I showed you from this  pathway sort of the relationship between  some of these factors and how they can  impact the overall success of an green  program and those are really my  conclusions so you want to consider all  of these factors you want to work your  way through these problems step-by-step  you start if at all possible from the  simple to the complex don't  automatically think it's some big  picture flashy problem because many  times it's something very very simple so  you really want to go simple to the  complex and then you want to apply new  data as it becomes available so  particularly over the last couple of  years we've been getting better and  better people are starting to look at  factors that impact reproductive success  whether it be diet whether it be  behavior welfare conditions all these  things and how they impact reproductive  success you should be keeping a sort of  abreast of these of these developments  as they come out and then use that  information to help inform your breeding  programs because it might give you this  talk next year I'm going to know more  and the talk is gonna be different okay  and so this should be the more we know  the better we can inform these processes  the better we can work our way through  problems when they come so last but not  least I'd like to thank the organizers  organizers of a conference live lab  roots everybody here at Boston  Children's and then people that have  shared with me materials and information  that I've used in these slides so what  I'd like to close with finally is please  if you have questions related to any of  this do not hesitate to ask me always  happy to answer them now thrilled if you  ask me those questions and send them to  me  always happy to talk so thank you very  much thank you for that informative  presentation unfortunately we are out of  time I want to thank the audience  reviewing today's event see you next  time goodbye
 

